  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Tuesday...\n\nBand of very elevated warm frontal light showers continues to \nclip Fallon County, but this activity should exit our area soon.\nMesoanalysis shows pwats have risen to 1-1.2\" and this will lead\nto convection developing over the southern mountains by around\n21z. Easterly winds and \"cooler\" temps will keep instability\ncapped across lower elevations, and without any real forcing\nexpect today's activity to remain confined to the high terrain.\nTonight, with a southeasterly LLJ, most CAMs show some weak storms\nover far southeast MT, which may linger to sunrise tomorrow.\nToday's storms will be capable of producing heavy rain and erratic\ngusts, but should remain sub-severe w/ bulk shear of 20kts or\nless.\n\nWednesday looks like the moistest day of the week w/ pwats closer\nto 1.5\". Also, there is a distinct wave that is expected to emerge\nfrom the northern great basin and track thru the region late \ntomorrow through tomorrow night. Again, severe wx is not expected \nper weak shear, but very heavy rain and erratic winds (wet \nmicrobursts possible) are risks. Storm coverage should be greatest\nnear mountains & foothills, and storm motion will be slow, so \nhave issued a Flood Watch for potential flash flooding across the \nSW/S (along and south of a line from Livingston to Columbus to \nLodge Grass). Burn scars such as Robertson Draw, East Side and Elk\nwill be particularly susceptible to flash flooding w/ debris \nflows. Timing of the Watch is from 1pm to 2am.\n\nWet thunderstorm activity will continue Thursday but the air mass\nwill begin to turn drier behind the exiting shortwave. Still a\nrisk of heavy rain but not to the extent of Wednesday. WPC has\nsouthern portions of the region highlighted for a low risk of \nexcessive rain each of the next two days. Stay alert if you have\noutdoor/recreation plans. Temps tomorrow and Thursday will remain\nseasonable with highs mostly mid 80s to lower 90s.\n\nThe air mass will turn notably drier by Friday as the exiting wave\nscours out the deep monsoonal moisture in place. That said, pwats\nwill still be near to slightly above normal, so there remains a \nslight (10-20%) chance of higher based t-storms each day through \nthe first part of next week. However, the main story is the strong\nridge expected to bring hotter & drier conditions starting \nFriday. Mixed west winds look to be breezy on Friday, with deep \nmixing expected to push temps back to triple digits. Along with \nhumidities in the teens, would expect fire concerns to be on the \nincrease again after about of week of elevated RHs. Triple digit \nhighs are likely again Saturday, though with less wind. The \nSunday/Monday time period may see a brief flattening of the ridge \nand possible weak cold front, thus something to monitor with \nregard to fire weather/wind. Overall, the longwave pattern thru \nnext week favors above normal heights and temperatures.\n\nFor those following the July 25th drama, the odd (and meaningless)\nfact that the Billings airport has never reached 100F on this date\ngoing back to 1934, confidence is growing that the site will \nfinally reach triple digits. Current forecast for Billings is \n103F, which would easily break the old record of 98F set on four \noccasions. The wildcard is smoke and high cloud cover, which could\nhold temps back slightly. Stay tuned.\n\nJKL",
